(a) Naming or renaming of a public right-of-way or facility shall occur only when the name is approved by a simple majority vote of the City Council, and the proposed naming or renaming satisfies at least one of the following criteria:
(1) It honors and commemorates a noteworthy person associated with the City of Hinesville, the State of Georgia, or the United State of America;
(2) It commemorates local history, places, events, or culture;
(3) It will strengthen neighborhood identity; or
(b) The following shall not be considered for purposes of naming or renaming:
(1) Persons that resided in the City of Hinesville for less than five years, other than prominent national figures;
(2) Names assigned to existing public property within the City or unincorporated Liberty County, where duplication of the name is likely to lead to confusion.
(c) Naming requests shall consider:
(1) The number of residents and businesses directly affected;
(2) Community diversity;
(3) The historical significance of preexisting name or location;
(4) The appropriateness and compatibility of the request in relation to the existing area;
(5) The impact on emergency service delivery; and
(6) Continuity and stability
(d) Request to name a public right-of way or facility in honor of a natural person should also consider the following:
(1) Absent extraordinary or extenuating circumstances, the proposed honoree shall be deceased;
(2) Whether the proposed namesake made a sustained contribution over an applicable time period, exerted efforts of benefit or significance to local, state, or national affairs and/or demonstrated leadership in government, cultural, or historical affairs, humanitarian efforts, or community development;
(3) Contributions that beneficially changed the nature and characteristics of the community; and
(4) Achievement by a native of Hinesville or of national or international acclaim in a specific field of endeavor that has reflected positively on Hinesville.
(Ord. No. 2023-11, 11-16-2023)

